      "text": "To find the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the unique permutations of these digits. The key point is that we are dealing with a set of digits where some are repeated.\n\nFirst, let's determine the total number of permutations of the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 without considering the repetition. The formula for the number of permutations of a set of items where some items are repeated is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the repeating items. In this case, we have 4 items in total (2, 2, 9, 9), so \\( n = 4 \\), \\( n_1 = 2 \\) (for the two 2's), and \\( n_2 = 2 \\) (for the two 9's). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the digits 2 and 9 are both used twice, we need to adjust for the fact that the order of the digits matters and we have symmetric arrangements. We can consider the permutations in steps:\n\n1. Choose the first digit. There are 2 choices (either 2 or 9).\n2. Choose the second digit. There are 2 choices remaining after choosing the first digit.\n3. Choose the third digit. There is 1 choice left after choosing the first two digits.\n4. The fourth digit is automatically determined.\n\nSo, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed is:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 2 \\times 1 \\times 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nTo list them explicitly, we can consider the different ways to arrange the digits:\n\n- 2299\n- 229_, _299\n- 22_99\n- 2_299\n- 2_992\n- _2299\n\nHowever, listing all permutations explicitly doesn't change the fundamental understanding. The correct count of unique permutations, considering the symmetry, is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
