      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the unique permutations of these digits. The number of unique permutations of a set of distinct digits is given by the formula for permutations of a multiset:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of permutations} = \\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this problem, we have four digits: 2, 2, 9, and 9. The total number of digits is 4, and the digit 2 appears twice, while the digit 9 also appears twice. Therefore, the number of unique permutations is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the factorials:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
